  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-2e1222dc37c7465e": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-689",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Jewish children, when very young, were taught to wave the branches of palm and boughs of myrtle and willow bound together, which were used at the Feast of Tabernacles; and also, while shaking them, to join in the chorus of Hosanna. It is a fact worth noticing, we think, that…"
      },
      "c-66e7ef971f09eb6d": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-688",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 11:15; Luke 19:45. In John 2:14 is an account of a similar occurrence which took place during the first year of Christ’s ministry. This temple market is supposed to have been established after the captivity, when many came from foreign lands to Jerusalem. Lightfoot…"
      },
      "c-7528158a3b9303b1": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-690",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 12:1; Luke 20:9. The vines are sometimes planted on the side of a terraced hill, the old branches being permitted to trail along the ground, while the fruit-bearing shoots are propped with forked sticks. An ancient mode of planting vineyards was by training the vine…"
      },
      "c-b6060247df4a1abf": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-687",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 11:8; Luke 19:35-36; John 12:18. The custom is still sometimes seen in the East. Roberts was surprised, shortly after his arrival in India, to find, on paying a visit to a native gentleman, that the path through the garden was covered with white garments on which he…"
      }
    },
